---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const pageTitle =
  '"Saint John": what is writing to this log file?"';
const unit = '"Saint-John"';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  unit={unit}
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">
    {pageTitle}
  </h1>
  <hr class="mb-6" />

  <!-- Scenario Explanation -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Scenario: Understanding the
      Problem
    </summary>
    <p>
      A developer’s program is
      continuously writing to
      <code>/var/log/bad.log</code>,
      filling up disk space. Your task:
      identify and stop this process. Do <strong
        >not</strong
      > delete the log file.
    </p>
    <ul
      class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 space-y-1"
    >
      <li>
        Check live output: <code
          >tail -f /var/log/bad.log</code
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        Goal: terminate the program so
        the file stops growing
      </li>
      <li>
        Root privileges are available,
        so use <code>sudo</code> if needed
      </li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <!-- Step 1: Identify the process -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Step 1: Find the culprit process
    </summary>
    <p>
      Use Linux tools to see which
      process is writing to the log:
    </p>
    <ul
      class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 space-y-1"
    >
      <li>
        <strong>lsof:</strong> Lists open
        files by process
        <pre
          class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>sudo lsof /var/log/bad.log</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>fuser:</strong> Shows PIDs
        using a file
        <pre
          class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>sudo fuser /var/log/bad.log</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>ps + grep:</strong> If you
        know the name of the program
        <pre
          class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>ps aux | grep bad.log</code></pre>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These commands will give you the
      PID(s) of the process writing to
      the file.
    </p>
  </details>

  <!-- Step 2: Terminate the process -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Step 2: Kill the process
    </summary>
    <ul
      class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 space-y-1"
    >
      <li>
        Once you have the PID (e.g.,
        1234), stop the process:
        <pre
          class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>sudo kill 1234</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        If the process ignores SIGTERM:
        <pre
          class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>sudo kill -9 1234</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        Verify the process is gone: <code
          >ps -p 1234</code
        > (should return nothing)
      </li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <!-- Step 3: Verify the log is no longer growing -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Step 3: Check the log file
    </summary>
    <p>
      Ensure the log file stops growing
      over time:
    </p>
    <pre
      class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>watch -n 2 ls -lh /var/log/bad.log</code></pre>
    <p>Or keep monitoring with tail:</p>
    <pre
      class="bg-gray-100 p-2 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>tail -f /var/log/bad.log</code></pre>
    <p>
      If no new lines appear after
      killing the process, the challenge
      is complete.
    </p>
  </details>

  <!-- Optional: Bonus tips -->
  <details
    class="mb-4 border rounded p-4"
  >
    <summary
      class="cursor-pointer font-semibold"
    >
      Bonus Tips
    </summary>
    <ul
      class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 space-y-1"
    >
      <li>
        Use <code>htop</code> or <code
          >top</code
        > for a live view of processes.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check if a cron job is
        recreating the process: <code
          >crontab -l</code
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        Use <code>systemctl status</code
        > if it’s running as a service.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </details>
</BaseLayout>
